hbo's succession is a great show about a  fictional media business  and there are some really interesting  scenes in season one that relate to  common real world situations  all of these clips come from season one  so if you haven't watched the full  season  there will be some spoilers in this  video you should go and watch it though  it's a really fun show and it's super  well produced  i'm john coogan and here's my reaction  to hbo's  succession now to be clear i've never  been on the board of a public company  but i have attended board meetings every  quarter for about the last decade  i've also never been involved in a  family business like waystar royco  in succession so i'm going to focus on  clips that i think generalize well to my  personal experience in startups  first up is a scene from the pilot  season 1 episode 1  celebration in this scene kendall roy is  trying to make a name for himself at the  company  by acquiring a small digital media  startup let's take a look at how he acts  during the deal  hey hey hey buddy good to see you  so we ready to [ __ ] you up  yeah okay look  i'm really sorry but it looks like  there's an issue  come on i came all the way up here dude  yeah i'm sorry  uh dude  okay listen you know i love what you do  right  i mean i'd love to keep you and your  team in place lawrence  i think vaulter is the shiz where the  shiz  yeah what's it gonna take you want me to  i'm gonna send a vintage jaguar over to  your house today  i'll do it so first up he's very  unprofessional and super casual here  it's not uncommon for people to try and  be friendly during a negotiation  especially if the lawyers aren't around  and you're talking about a deal  just founder of founder but this clearly  backfires  you should always try and act as  professional as possible when you're  early in a deal process  selling a company can be very emotional  so having empathy for what the founder  is going through goes a long way  kendall is all about putting pressure on  the deal and he takes it a step further  later in the episode the number is 140  cash with a stock alternative  you're on our board take the stock  you'll own a nice piece of us  well that is an appealing package  it is yeah it's appealing easy you still  need me to recommend this  no i don't this is a deal so [ __ ]  good you have to take it  or we'll see you in court dude  raising the acquisition price is a great  way to get a deal done if you are  struggling to find a shared vision  at this point it looks like kendall has  offered a high enough price that the  investors basically can't say no  now there is one oddity here he says if  you don't take the deal  we'll see you in court generally if a  founder blocks a deal  that would benefit the shareholders they  can't really be sued by the company  that's trying to acquire them  they could get sued by the shareholders  since all board members  including founders have a fiduciary duty  to shareholders  but if westar royco doesn't own any  shares in the target company  they can't really sue them for turning  down the deal applying pressure to close  an acquisition  does happen though usually it just takes  the form of threatening to launch a  competing product  this is what happened when facebook  acquired instagram kevin systrom the  founder of instagram  asked a friend who knew mark zuckerberg  personally what would happen if  instagram turned down facebook's  acquisition offer  system's friend said that zuckerberg  would quote go into destroy mode  direct competition like that can be  really scary for an early stage startup  so that's usually where the pressure  gets applied the second scene i want to  cover is from episode 6  which side are you on kendall is trying  to mount a coup  and get the board to issue a vote of no  confidence  boardroom conflicts are already  extremely dramatic but the writers  heighten  everything by making kendall run late  for the meeting one of his allies tries  to stall for time  let's take a look it's 12 noon  and i'd like to call the meeting of  waystar  royco to order roll call what  logo roy yeah you're here done roll call  what is wrong we're doing it today  why are we doing roll calls because  that's how we do it right noah  so logan roy is here  frank vernon present kendall roy  absent roman roy yo  a formal roll call like this is  basically unheard of and it's a pretty  blatant attempt to stall for time  usually the lawyers who are taking  minutes at the meeting would just look  around the room and make a note in the  minutes of who is in attendance  if your lawyers are formally calling  roll they're probably just trying to pad  out their timesheets and bill you more  just don't do it every company usually  has a set of rules that govern who can  call a board meeting and under what  circumstances  special meetings can be called so this  vote of no confidence doesn't  necessarily have to take place at this  particular meeting  but there are some restrictions usually  you would need the majority of the board  to agree to a meeting and schedule it a  few days in advance  each meeting requires a quorum for any  decisions to be legally valid  this prevents a situation where a board  waits until a particular board member is  traveling internationally without cell  phone service  and then calls a meeting instantly and  makes decisions without them  this is kind of a spoiler but the coup  fails and kendall is immediately fired  i'm chairman casting vote i win it's  [ __ ] over  what's the vote my son  that was your best shot you lost i think  there's some doubt as to whether he lost  one  kendall frank asha alona off the board  fired with immediate defense i don't  think you're able to security i am in  the middle of turning a [ __ ]  house you're what i call security i have  some doubt i'm in the middle  of turning up tanker  now a ceo logan roy can fire any members  of the management team  so it's very realistic that he would  fire kendall on the spot for leading the  coup  what's a little odd is that he also  fires several other board members at the  same time  typically board members earn their seats  through investments  and the investment firm they represent  is actually legally entitled to a seat  a good example of this in practice took  place during the uber controversy of  2017.  after facing a number of metoo related  culture issues  uber held a company-wide meeting where  the board presented  their thinking about a way forward at  the meeting david bonderman  an uber board member and a partner at  the private equity firm tpg  made some impolite remarks about women  and was quickly pressured to resign  but tpg still had rights to the board  seat so they installed david trujillo as  bonderman's replacement  replacing a board member who represents  a financial firm will not  likely change the way that seat votes on  important issues  and if you're an independent director  who can be removed by the chairman at  any time  it would be extremely difficult to vote  against that chairman so  this scenario is a little odd to me the  last clip i want to talk about  is from episode 8 prague in this scene  kendall is trying to rebound from his  firing at waystar  by getting into startup investing he's  clearly out of his element and it really  shows when he goes to pitch an art  dealing startup called dust  handle this is dust hey  great to connect love the name by the  way very  funky uh sorry please excuse captain  beef up here hey  how's it going how's it going cool  earrings very uh bauhaus  big fan of what you guys do sweet chili  sauce  thanks uh likewise  angela you want to spin us through the  pitch deck sure  so that's okay i don't need to hear the  pitch i've been through the deck  and uh i get it basically uh you buy a  painting from  some art student in a basement jack up  the price sell it to some morgan stanley  sex pest and you me and the student i'll  get rich right  we're interested in increasing the reach  of young artists  and the democratization of art no  absolutely  i get it you're uh providing a platform  to marginalize talent that the next  generation  of artists i'm just telling you how it  works from the  outside to the sharks most founders tend  to really believe in their missions  so coldly describing how you could  hollow that mission out  and focus purely on profits is never a  good strategy for an early stage  investor  it's fine to talk about how the business  model will evolve after all most  founders of for-profit companies want to  build companies  that fulfill their missions while making  money but ignoring the real world impact  that a startup can have is a big mistake  the bigger issue here to me is that  kendall is clearly trying to  self-actualize through his startup  investing  and that's never a good sign jeff lewis  the founder of bedrock capital  has a great youtube clip describing why  this is so important  you want to work with a vc who is not  trying to self-actualize  through investing in you and this is  basically the number one vector  on which you want to optimize for when  you're selecting a venture capital  partner  it's not firm brand it's not value ad  it's not  track record it is someone who seems as  if they are self-actualized  and does not need their investment in  your company to be a mechanism  for them to self-actualize why is that  because it's basically impossible to  self-actualize through making an  investment  okay so there are lots of ways to  self-actualize in the world  but core to self-actualizing means being  in control  of whatever the vector of  self-actualization is so  if if you're an entrepreneur you can  self-actualize through building your  company  if you're a vc who's founded your own vc  firm you can self-actualize through that  but you can't self-actualize through  writing someone a check and giving them  advice  investors who haven't built anything for  themselves can wind up exerting too much  control  because they want your company to be the  thing that they are personally known for  and that's never good  later in the episode the founder of dust  explains why she is turning down  kendall's investment offer  what happened he told me i'm sorry it's  nothing personal  i i shouldn't be telling you this but  i'm about to land a chunk of seed  capital the size of  like idaho so i mean whatever it is you  need  i can make it happen okay yeah okay  okay so are we good  you know it's just not about that  then like help me out here because  there's something i'm not seeing  it's the name which you mean dust  roy dendelroy it'd be like i was  marrying hitler and then i'd get to be  what mrs hitler  okay uh that's not really fair i mean  look that's that's not me i'm not  a roy well i would personally avoid  working with kendall in this situation  because he doesn't seem like a  self-actualized vc  and would probably be overbearing the  founder cites  the business practices and controversial  political leanings of waystar as the  determining factor  political alignment is increasingly  becoming a reason why founders choose to  work with specific vc firms  shortly after the storming of the united  states capital i saw a tweet by hunter  walk  who runs the seed stage firm homebrew  advising entrepreneurs  to not take money from jared kushner who  serves as a senior advisor to president  trump  who is his father-in-law not all  founders will have the luxury of turning  investors away based on their political  beliefs  but it's not unreasonable to try and  find venture capitalists who see eye to  eye with you on how the future should  unfold  politics continues to dominate every  aspect of public life  and you don't want your board meetings  to turn into political debates  that just won't be productive but what  do you think did you enjoy succession  let me know in the comments if you've  seen the show and what you liked about  it  and if you enjoyed the video please  consider subscribing to the channel  thanks for watching
 

